• Now the experiment is clean:

  - RUN A (mutant, fixture where signatures appear nowhere in `## Error patterns`, committed counter copy = mutant bytes): ALL 8 legs PASS, exit 0. → The SIGNATURES leg is the only thing enforcing the linkage; dropping it lets a table whose signatures appear nowhere in `## Error patterns` pass every other leg (SECTION/TABLE/STATES/FIELDS/EVIDENCE/SOURCE all pass).
  - RUN B (original counter, same tree, committed copy = original bytes): SIGNATURES FAIL with all five names missing → the leg is load-bearing; without mutation the same fixture fails.
  - RUN C (mutant, committed copy = original bytes): STAMP FAIL (23efe0c0… vs 06b0352a…) → tamper-evident: skipping via a mutated counter is detected as long as the committed counter copy is intact. In the real lane, reports/round-69/accept-d.py is committed (HEAD 6a98d7d, clean vs index, sha256 06b0352a…), so a skip requires mutating a committed production file, which jj/git would show as a working-copy change vs. the declared revision.

  Also RUN D: unmodified selftest: GREEN PASS 8/8, DO-NOTHING FAIL as required 1/8, SELFTEST 2/2, exit 0.

  Extra observation worth reporting (design note, not a defect): SIGNATURES uses substring matching (`r[3] not in ep_text`) against the whole `## Error patterns` section text — so in the unmodified counter, the linkage is satisfiable by ANY occurrence of the name anywhere inside that section (even one comma-separated line listing all five, without any observation/reading row). Also, if the `## Error patterns` heading is absent entirely, ep_text="" and every non-empty signature fails — good. Also note: the header cell "Error signature" itself — if a signature name were, say, "signature", substring collisions could false-pass; the five names are distinctive enough.

  Another nuance for the report: the SOURCE leg prints S859 five times (one per row) — cosmetic.

  Also the STAMP detail string bug-ish: when committed file is missing, detail prints "committed=none" — that's fine.

  One more possible finding: STAMP compares the running file's hash to the tree copy — it does NOT anchor to the git revision. If a worker edits BOTH the running counter and the committed tree copy (both uncommitted working-copy mutations in the real lane... wait, in the real lane the counter IS committed at HEAD 6a98d7d; the tree copy equals HEAD; any edit shows in jj status as a working-copy change). So the enforcement anchor is really the VCS, and STAMP only ties the executed counter to the tree copy. Good to state precisely.

