  Findings:
  1. VERDICT: PASS — linkage skippable by counter mutation: SIGNATURES is the sole leg touching `## Error patterns`; dropping it passes 8/8 on a signature-free-of-Error-patterns tree.
  2. VERDICT: PASS (control) — unmodified counter kills the same tree (SIGNATURES missing=all five) → leg load-bearing; the pass in RUN A is attributable to the mutation alone.
  3. VERDICT: PASS (tamper-evidence) — STAMP detects the mutated counter whenever the committed copy at reports/round-69/accept-d.py is intact; that file is committed (clean vs index; sha 06b0352a…), so a silent skip would have to mutate a committed production byte, visible to jj/git. Not tamper-proof against someone who rewrites both the runner and the tree copy AND commits — but that's an auditable production-file change outside a worker's write scope this round.
  4. Answer: The error-signature linkage can be skipped only by editing the counter and replacing the committed counter copy — a visible working-copy mutation of a committed production file. Against the frozen counter it cannot be skipped: RUN C fails at STAMP.
  5. Design notes (non-blocking):
     - SIGNATURES matching is substring-based over the whole `## Error patterns` section: any occurrence of each name anywhere in the section satisfies it (e.g. one line listing all five names with no table rows). Weakest enforcement form; the S-row/O2-style structure isn't checked there.
     - If `## Error patterns` heading is absent, ep_text="" and the leg fails for any non-empty signature (good).
